Entry number 4 
Alright so for this entry im going to talk about how I actually restarted my progress.
I realized that all the time I wasted un tangleing my pcb could just have easily been prevented if I didnt make a silly formating mistake in my schematic phase.
But now that I know basically all the steps and im properly familar with the program KICAD I was certain that I would be able to fix all of my mistakes and just make a better overall keyboard pcb.
I swapped the layout to a basic 60% keyboard to save enough GPIO pins on my raspberry pico for an oled monitor on the side and I made sure to deep comb through all of my prior mistakes to make sure I didnt repeat them.
I also got to the routing step surprisingly fast once I started working actually knowing what I was doing in the program.
I spent a decent amount of time making traces on the front and the back of the pcb making sure to take advantage of via's to overlap some of the routes. 
also once everything was connected (which I ensured to never have a larger that 45 degree angle) I did a fill zone to ground the board to finish it off to make it look more proffesional 
